{title: Leaning on the Everlasting Arms}
{artist: Elisha A. Hoffman}
{key: D}
{tempo: 108}
{time: 4/4}
{meta: tune SHOWALTER - Anthony J. Showalter (1887)}
{meta: public_domain Text 1887, tune 1887: both published before 1931 (US public domain); all authors, translators and composers died over 70 years ago. Chords and section layout: original simple arrangement by Tabernacle Tools. Original verses only, no modern additions.}

{start_of_verse: Verse 1}
What a [D]fellowship, what a joy divine, [G]leaning on the ever[D]lasting arms;
What a blessedness, what a peace is mine, [A]leaning on the ever[D]lasting arms.
{end_of_verse}
{start_of_chorus: Chorus}
[D]Leaning, leaning, [G]safe and secure from all a[D]larms;
[D]Leaning, leaning, [A]leaning on the ever[D]lasting arms.
{end_of_chorus}
{start_of_verse: Verse 2}
O how [D]sweet to walk in this pilgrim way, [G]leaning on the ever[D]lasting arms;
O how bright the path grows from day to day, [A]leaning on the ever[D]lasting arms.
{end_of_verse}
{start_of_verse: Verse 3}
What have [D]I to dread, what have I to fear, [G]leaning on the ever[D]lasting arms?
I have blessed peace with my Lord so near, [A]leaning on the ever[D]lasting arms.
{end_of_verse}
